[{"page":1,"text":"NOTICE OF A REGULAR\n\nCITY COUNCIL MEETING\n\nAugust 11, 2026, at 6:00 PM\n\nPresent:\n\nMayor Zack Stratton\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Holdaway arrived 6:05 pm\n\nCouncil Member David Lauret\n\nCouncil Member Parker McCumber\n\nCouncil Member Ezra Nair\n\nAbsent:\n\nCouncil Member Jacob Wood\nStaff Present: Building Director Cris Johnson, Chief Deputy Holden Rockwell with the Utah\nCounty Sherrif’s Office, City Attorney Jesse Riddle City Manager Brian Voeks, City Recorder\nRobin Bond, Community Enrichment Director Brian Vawdrey, Deputy City Recorder Tony\nLara, Deputy Mayor David Herring, Finance Director Evan Smith, Media Specialist Braile\nTyler, Public Works Coordinator Dareli Villegas, and Public Works Director/City Engineer\n\nNaseem Ghandour.\nAlso Present: Marshal Jones, David Pearce, Kim Smith, Ryan Bybee, Malerie Walker, Tyler\nTinsdell, Jane Pearce, Emmeline McCumber, Trinity Ewing, Davie Hyte, Malerie Walker,\nRachael Stratton, Evelyn Stratton, Laley Winkel, and others who did not sign in or whose\nhandwriting was illegible.\n\nAGENDA\n\n1. CALL TO ORDER\n\nMayor Stratton called the meeting to order at 6:00 pm.\n\n2. INVOCATION AND PLEDGE OF ALLEGIANCE (BY INVITATION)\n\nMiss Evelyn Stratton gave the prayer and led the pledge.\n\n3.\n\nPUBLIC HEARINGS\n\n3.1. Public Hearing for an Amendment of Vineyard City Subdivision Code\nMayor Stratton opened the public hearing at 6:01 pm. No comments were made. Mayor Stratton\nclosed the public hearing at 6:02 pm.\n\n3.2. Public Hearing for Proposed Amendment to the Vineyard Consolidated Fee\n\nSchedule Establishing a $150 Insignificant Land Disposal Application Fee\nMayor Stratton opened the public hearing at 6:02 pm. The public made no comments. Council\nMember Nair wished to lower the application fee to $50. Staff indicated there were no objections\nto a lower fee amount. Consensus of the Council favored a $50 non-refundable application fee.\n\nMayor Stratton closed the public hearing at 6:04 pm.\nPage 1 of 5; August 11, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"4.\n\nPUBLIC COMMENTS (3 MINUTES)\n“Public Comments” is defined as time set aside for citizens to express their views. Public\ncomments can be submitted ahead of time to robinr@vineyardutah.gov.\nMarshall Jones addressed the Council regarding short-term rental regulations. He explained\nthat he entered into a purchase agreement for his Vineyard home in April 2017, prior to the\nCity's prohibition of short-term rentals, and completed the purchase shortly after the regulation\nchanged. Mr. Jones requested consideration of his unique circumstances and asked the Council\nto review whether an exception or grandfathering provision may be appropriate based on the\ntiming of his purchase and intended use of the property. The Myor thanked Mr. Jones for his\ncomments and indicated staff would follow up.\nTyler Tinsdell spoke in support of Mr. Jones' request, stating that real estate transactions\nand disclosures at the time of purchase reflected that short-term rentals were permitted. He\nencouraged the Council to consider the circumstances under which the property was purchased\nwithout creating broader precedent.\n\nKim Smith, Elms, representing residents and parents from The Elms neighborhood,\naddressed concerns regarding pedestrian safety along 400 South and the school walking route.\nShe expressed appreciation for the City's ongoing efforts, including discussions with school\nofficials and law enforcement, and emphasized the importance of prioritizing student safety. Ms.\nSmith encouraged the Council to support roadway and pathway improvements that provide safe\naccess for children walking to school.\n\nRyan Bybee discussed the 400 South roadway alignment and presented a proposed\nalternative design. He stated the proposal would maintain vehicle travel lanes, bicycle\naccommodations, pedestrian access, and safety buffers while avoiding impacts to adjacent\nprivate property. Mr. Bybee indicated the design had been reviewed from an engineering\nperspective and requested further discussion with City staff regarding feasibility and costs.\nCouncil Member Nair asked about the certifications of the drawing and changing the lines\nof the road.\nMalerie Walker commented on proposed modifications to the design of 400 South. She\nexpressed support for maintaining the city-approved design, including dedicated bicycle lanes\nand roadway width necessary to accommodate future growth, transportation needs, and public\nsafety. She emphasized the importance of preserving long-term transportation infrastructure and\nensuring adequate separation between vehicles, cyclists, and pedestrians.\nDavid Pearce thanked the city council for their service. Mr. Pearce also commented on the\n400 South roadway project, noting that the approved design had been developed over several\nyears and aligned with the City's transportation planning efforts. He seconded Malerie Walker’s\ncomments. He liked the original city plans. He expressed support for maintaining the planned\nroadway improvements and emphasized the long-term benefits of constructing the roadway as\noriginally designed.\nPage 2 of 5; August 11, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"Public comment was then closed.\n\n5.\n\nCONSENT ITEMS\n\n5.1. Approval of the July 7, 2026, City Council Meeting Minutes\n\n5.2. Approval of the July 14th, 2026, City Council Meeting Minutes\n\n5.3. Approval of the August 4, 2026, City Council Meeting Minutes\n\n5.4. Approve Ordinance 2026-07 Amending Municipal Code for the Planning\n\nDepartment (Anthony Fletcher)\n\nItems 5.4 and 5.5 were removed from the Consent Items and discussed by Council.\nMOTION: Council Member Lauret motioned to approve the Consent Items 5.1, 5.2, and 5.3 as\npresented. Council Member Nair seconded the motion. Vote Yes: Council Members Holdaway,\n\nLauret, McCumber, and Nair No: None. Motion carried 4-0.\nCouncil Member Lauret asked for clarification on the municipal planning code, Item 5.4.\nMr. Ghandour provided an overview of the proposed code amendment relating to plat signatures\nand administrative processes. The amendment would remove references to the former \"City\nPlanner\" position and allow designated planning staff to sign plats on behalf of the Land Use\n\nAuthority after approval by the Development Review Committee.\n.\nMotion: Council Member Nair moved approve item 5.4 as presented. (Ordinance 2026-07)\n\nCouncil Member McCumber seconded the motion. Roll Call Vote. Yes: Council Members\nMcCumber and Nair. No: Council Members Holdaway and Lauret. Tie Vote resolved by Mayor\n\nStratton’s vote of Yes. Motion Passed 3-2.\n5.5. Approve Resolution 2026-34 Amending the Consolidated Fee Schedule to include an\n\nInsignificant Land Disposal Application Fee (Evan Smith)\nMotion: Council Member Nair motioned to approve 5.5 with an amendment to change the $150\nfee with the $75 refundable portion to $50 with no refundable portion. Seconded by Council\n\nMember McCumber. Roll Call Vote. Yes: Council Members Holdaway, Lauret, McCumber,\nand Nair. No: None. Motion passed 4-0.\n\n6.\n\nCLOSED SESSION\nThe Mayor and City Council pursuant to Utah Code 52-4-205 may vote to go into a closed\nsession for the purpose of litigation.\nMOTION: Council Member McCumber motioned to go into a closed session to discuss pending\nor reasonably imminent litigation. Council Member Lauret seconded the motion. Roll Call Vote\nYes: Nair, McCumber, and Lauret. No: None. Recused: Council Member Holdaway. Motion\ncarried 3-0.\n\n7. ADJOURNMENT\nMayor Stratton adjourned the meeting at 6:35 pm. Council went into closed session.\n\nPage 3 of 5; August 11, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"MINUTES APPROVED ON: August 25, 2026\n\nCERTIFIED BY:\n\n_____ __________________________\n\nRobin Bond, Vineyard City Recorder\n\nPage 4 of 5; August 11, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"City Council\n\nClose Session Meeting\n\nAugust 11, 2026\n\nIn Attendance:\n\nMayor Zack Stratton\n\nCouncilmember Parker McCumber\n\nCouncilmember Jacob Holdaway withdrew at 6:55 pm. Returned at 7:19 pm\n\nCouncilmember David Lauret\n\nCouncilmember Ezra Nair\nStaff: City Attorney Jesse Riddle, City Manager Brian Voeks, City Recorder Robin Bond, and\n\nDeputy Mayor David Kyle Herring.\n\nStart: 6:38 pm.\nMOTION: Council Member McCumber motioned to close the closed meeting. Council Member\nLauret seconded the motion. All Yes: Council Member Holdaway, Lauret, McCumber, and\n\nNair. No: None. Motion carried 4-0.\n\nEnded 7:48 pm.\n\nMINUTES APPROVED ON:\n\nCERTIFIED (NOTICED) BY:\n\nPage 5 of 5; August 11, 2026, City Council Agenda\n\nAugust 25, 2026\nROBIN BOND, CITY RECORDER","ocr":false}]